cartridge "CONSTRUCTION CO./B.W. BEHLING/1984"

' CONSTRUCTION CO.
' A ColecoVision game based on "Construction Co." by B.W. Behling, Ahoy!
' 1984 (Commodore 64 BASIC, file CONST.CO on the unofficial Ahoy! July-
' December 1984 disk; author and year from its title screen, the issue
' month not found; orig/).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game (the original's rules, read from its listing, orig/constco.bas):
' you contract to build 1 to 5 houses. You start with $35,000, all of it a
' bank loan: $39,200 (12% interest) is due on day 30. Every morning the
' day advances; one morning in 250 thieves take every material you have
' stocked (never on day 1), and one in 250 you are arrested for bribing a
' building inspector: no work that day. When a loan falls due it is paid
' from your cash. With no cash left (0 or less) you are bankrupt. With no
' loan and less than $5,000, the bank offers $15,000, $30,000 or $45,000
' at 12%, due in 30 days. You hire 1 to 9 workers for the day (8 hours
' each, $70 a worker a day) and buy materials at the day's prices: each
' material has a base price and a random mark-up (bricks $0.15 + up to
' $0.60 each ... garage doors $300 + up to $200); below half the mark-up
' it is ON SALE. Then the crew works. The house goes up in the original's
' twelve stages with its quantities: brickwork (10 bricks and 8 lb of
' cement a tenth of an hour, 1010 times), framing (3 boards, 3/4 hour,
' 534 times), roofing (10 shingles an hour, 81 times), plumbing and
' wiring (3 pipes and 7 ft of wire an hour, 167 times), siding (3 boards,
' 3/4 hour, 223 times), plastering (3 lb, 1/2 hour, 267 times), windows
' (1 window, 1 1/2 hours, 25 times), fence (4 boards, 1/2 hour, 28 times),
' doors (1 door, 2 hours, 18 times), the garage door (one, 42 quarter
' hours), sidewalk (10 lb of cement, 1 1/2 hours, 10 times) and painting
' (1 gallon, 4 hours, 50 times). The day ends when the crew has worked
' its hours; finishing a stage (all but fence and doors) also ends the
' day, as in the original. Running out of a material sends the crew home,
' paid for the whole day. A finished house sells for $50,000. At the end
' (all houses built, or bankrupt) your debts are paid from your cash:
' AFTER PAYING YOUR DEBTS YOU MADE $... CONGRATULATIONS!!! (a win), or
' TOO BAD YOU OWE THE BANK $... AND HAD TO RETIRE IN DISGRACE (a loss).
' The profit is the score; the best one is kept on the title.
'
' From the original: all the rules and numbers above, its messages, the
' materials and their price ranges, the house drawn as the stages go up
' (brick foundation and chimney, bare studs and rafters, shingles,
' siding, windows, fence, doors, garage door, sidewalk, paint), the side
' panel (day, hours, cash, crew).
'
' Liberties:
' - Joystick menus instead of typed numbers and Y/N keys: the morning
'   office (crew, loan, materials, start), and a supply yard with every
'   material on one page; the stick sets the amount (faster as you hold
'   it), the right button sets it to what the house still needs.
' - A NEED column (what the rest of this house still needs beyond your
'   stock) and the stage's progress bar: a first-time player can't know
'   the original's hidden quantities, and guessing them only made the
'   crew go home. The decisions stay: crew size against the day lost at
'   each stage end, buying on sale days against theft, cash for the
'   crew's pay, the loan's due date.
' - Money in whole dollars: a purchase costs its price times the amount,
'   rounded up to the dollar. Stock up to 60000 of each (original 99999).
' - Materials are checked before they are used (the original used them,
'   went negative, then sent the crew home); the totals are the same.
' - Bugs fixed: an arrest on the loan's due day skipped the payment for
'   good (now it is paid the next morning); the crew of a house's last
'   day was never paid (now it is).
' - Holding a button makes the crew work four times as fast on screen.
'   The house picture is redrawn for the ColecoVision's safe area (19 x 17
'   cells instead of 27 x 21), revealed in proportion to each stage's
'   work, with workers hammering. Sounds, a title tune, results that wait
'   for a button instead of delay loops, no play-again key (house ending).
'
' Controls: stick up/down picks a line, left/right changes a value, either
' button acts (holding one during work speeds it up); in the supply yard
' the right button sets the amount to what is needed. Either controller.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off after a
' wait, display off. Music first in the sound table; the title tune played
' once. `set sprite count` always given. Own xorshift random numbers.
' Nothing flashes faster than 3 Hz.

